<problem>
<!-- <titleanalytic stability of ODE equilibria, dx/dt=exponential of quadratic</title> -->

<setup>

<selectFromSequence name="a" from="-10" to=" 10" exclude="0" numToSelect="2" withReplacement="true" />
<selectFromSequence name="b" from="-10" to=" 10" numToSelect="3" withReplacement="true" />


<select name="x">x y z u v w p X Y Z S</select>
<function name="quadratic" variables="$x" symbolic="true"><math simplify="numbers">$a[1] *$x ^2+$b[1] *$x +$b[2]</math></function>
<function name="fun" variables="$x" symbolic="true"><math simplify="numbers">$a[2] *e^($$quadratic($x ))</math></function>


<mathList name="Es" />


</setup>



<p>Consider the 
  <select>
    <option>differential equation</option>
    <option>dynamical system</option>
  </select>
<md>
  <mrow>
   <select name="derivString">
     <option>\frac{ d$x }{ dt }</option>
     <option>$x'(t)</option>
   </select>
  \amp = <math extend="$fun.formula" /></mrow>
  <mrow>$x _0\amp = $b[3] </mrow>
  <mrow />
</md>
</p>
<p>
Find all the equilibria and determine their stability.
</p>


<p>Number of equilibria: <mathInput name="n_e" /></p>

<p>The equilibria are</p>
<ul>
  <repeatForSequence length="min(5, $n_e)" name="mapEs"><li><m>$(x)_e = </m> <mathInput /></li></repeatForSequence>
</ul>
<answer>
<award>
  <when>
    $n_e = 0 
  </when>
</award>
</answer>

<p>Stability of equilibria:</p>
<ul>
  <repeat for="$collectedEs" name="mapStab" valueName="v">
      <li><m>$(x)_e = $v</m>:
      <choiceInput inline="true">
        <choice>stable</choice>
        <choice>unstable</choice>
      </choiceInput>
      </li>
    </repeat>
</ul>
  
<answer maxNumAttempts="3">
  <award>
    <when>
      $n_e = 0 
    </when>
  </award>
</answer>



<solution>
<p>To find equilibria, set <m>$derivString = 0</m>.  If we let <m>f($x ) =<math extend="$fun.formula" /></m>, then an equilibrium <m>$x _e</m> must satisfy <m>f($x _e)=0</m>, or
<me>$$fun($(x)_e) =0.</me>
However, the exponential function can never be zero, so there are no solutions for real values of <m>$x </m>.   There are no equilibria.</p>

</solution>


</problem>